    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-uttara-kanda-0013",
      "sequence": 996,
      "meaning": "The Vedas praise Rama as the incomparable crown of kings whose form is both with attributes and beyond attributes, destroyer of Ravana and other fierce night-rangers, protector of the surrendered and compassionate Lord united with Shakti. Gods, demons, serpents, humans and all creation wander under his difficult maya, bound by time, action and qualities; those on whom he looks with mercy are freed from the threefold suffering. Those intoxicated with pride in knowledge who do not honour devotion fall even after attaining a rank rare to the gods, whereas servants who abandon every other hope and trust him cross becoming effortlessly by repeating his name. His lotus feet—worshipped by Shiva and Brahma, whose dust redeemed Ahalya and whose nails released the purifying Ganga—were pierced by forest thorns; those twin feet they worship always. They salute the beginningless tree of worldly existence, rooted in the unmanifest, with scriptural bark, six trunks, twenty-five branches, countless leaves and flowers, paired bitter and sweet fruit, and a single dependent vine. Though contemplatives meditate on the unborn nondual Brahman beyond mind, the Vedas ask to remain singers of Rama's manifest glory, loving his feet in thought, word and deed. After their prayer the Vedas disappear to Brahma's abode; Shiva then arrives before Raghubira, voice choked and body thrilled.",
